An idler gear is a gear that is inserted between the driver and the driven gear to change the direction of rotation. It does not affect the overall speed ratio of the gear train. From the figure: Gear 1 is the driver . Gears 2 and 3 are mounted on the same shaft (compound gear). Gear 3 drives Gear 4. Gear 4 drives Gear 5 (the driven gear). The overall speed ratio is

[latex]
\dfrac{\omega_5}{\omega_1}=
\dfrac{N_1}{N_2}
\times
\dfrac{N_3}{N_4}
\times
\dfrac{N_4}{N_5}
=
\dfrac{N_1N_3}{N_2N_5}
[/latex] Notice that [latex]N_4[/latex] cancels out completely. Therefore, Gear 4 does not affect the speed ratio and serves only to transmit motion and change the direction of rotation. Hence, Gear 4 is the idler gear.

The beam is massless and carries a concentrated mass of 5 kg at the free end. Therefore, it can be modeled as a spring-mass system. Equivalent stiffness at the free end due to a unit load on the overhang: [latex]
\delta=\frac{PL^2a^2}{3EI}
\quad\text{for}\quad
L=3\,\text{m},\; a=2\,\text{m}
[/latex] Using the standard result for the given overhanging beam, [latex]
\delta=\frac{4P}{EI}
[/latex] Hence, [latex]
k=\frac{P}{\delta}
=\frac{EI}{4}
\frac{1}{}
\;=\;50\ \text{N/m}
[/latex] Substituting [latex]
E=200\times10^9\ \text{N/m}^2,
\qquad
I=10^{-8}\ \text{m}^4
[/latex] [latex]
EI=2000\ \text{N\,m}^2
[/latex] Therefore,

[latex]
k=\frac{2000}{4}=500\ \text{N/m}
[/latex] The natural frequency is [latex]
\omega_n=\sqrt{\frac{k}{m}}
=\sqrt{\frac{500}{5}}
=\sqrt{100}
=10\ \text{rad/s}
[/latex]

